---
title: "RabbitMQ: consuming a queue into the event pipeline"
description: A BackgroundService that bridges a RabbitMQ queue into IEventMediator — the envelope, a type allow-list, scope-per-delivery dispatch, and an honest ack policy.
---

Ergosfare is an in-process mediator; it does not talk to brokers. That is a feature: the
broker client stays a thin adapter at the edge, and everything behind `PublishAsync` —
covariant handler matching, interceptors, groups, execution context — applies to a message
that arrived over the wire exactly as it applies to one raised in process.

This recipe builds that adapter for RabbitMQ: a `BackgroundService` that consumes a queue
and publishes each delivery into the event pipeline. It uses the async-first
`RabbitMQ.Client` 7.x API.

```bash
dotnet add package RabbitMQ.Client
```

## The envelope

A queue carries bytes, so something on the wire has to say which event a delivery is.
A minimal envelope carries the event's name and its serialized payload:

```csharp
public sealed record EventEnvelope(string EventType, JsonElement Payload);
```

`JsonElement` keeps the payload as parsed JSON instead of a doubly-encoded string — one
parse for the envelope, one targeted deserialization for the payload, no intermediate
`string` allocation.

## The type allow-list

Do not resolve `EventType` with `Type.GetType(...)`. A queue is an input boundary:
resolving arbitrary type names from message content lets anyone who can write to the queue
pick which types your process instantiates, and it breaks under trimming, where unreferenced
types no longer exist. An explicit allow-list solves both:

```csharp
public sealed class WireEventRegistry
{
    private readonly Dictionary<string, Type> _types;

    public WireEventRegistry(params Type[] eventTypes)
        => _types = eventTypes.ToDictionary(static t => t.Name, static t => t);

    public Type? Resolve(string eventType) => _types.GetValueOrDefault(eventType);
}
```

Every type in the list is referenced by a `typeof` at registration, so the trimmer keeps it
and the boundary only ever materializes events you chose.

## The consumer

The skeleton: one connection and channel for the service's lifetime, a durable queue, and
manual acks — the broker must not consider a delivery done before the pipeline ran.

```csharp
public sealed class RabbitMqEventConsumer(
    IServiceScopeFactory scopeFactory,
    WireEventRegistry wireEvents,
    IConnectionFactory connectionFactory,
    string queueName) : BackgroundService
{
    protected override async Task ExecuteAsync(CancellationToken stoppingToken)
    {
        await using var connection = await connectionFactory.CreateConnectionAsync(stoppingToken);
        await using var channel = await connection.CreateChannelAsync(cancellationToken: stoppingToken);

        await channel.QueueDeclareAsync(queueName, durable: true, exclusive: false,
            autoDelete: false, cancellationToken: stoppingToken);

        var consumer = new AsyncEventingBasicConsumer(channel);
        consumer.ReceivedAsync += (_, delivery) => HandleDeliveryAsync(channel, delivery, stoppingToken);

        await channel.BasicConsumeAsync(queueName, autoAck: false, consumer, stoppingToken);

        await Task.Delay(Timeout.Infinite, stoppingToken);
    }
}
```

## Dispatching a delivery

The interesting part. Piece by piece:

```csharp
private async Task HandleDeliveryAsync(
    IChannel channel, BasicDeliverEventArgs delivery, CancellationToken ct)
{
    try
    {
        var envelope = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<EventEnvelope>(delivery.Body.Span);

        if (envelope is null
            || wireEvents.Resolve(envelope.EventType) is not { } eventType
            || envelope.Payload.Deserialize(eventType) is not IEvent @event)
        {
            // Unknown or malformed: requeueing can only fail again. Nack without
            // requeue and let a dead-letter exchange keep the evidence.
            await channel.BasicNackAsync(delivery.DeliveryTag, multiple: false,
                requeue: false, cancellationToken: ct);
            return;
        }

        using var scope = scopeFactory.CreateScope();
        var events = scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<IEventMediator>();

        var settings = new EventMediationSettings();
        settings.Items["rabbitmq:deliveryTag"] = delivery.DeliveryTag;
        settings.Items["rabbitmq:redelivered"] = delivery.Redelivered;

        await events.PublishAsync(@event, settings, ct);

        await channel.BasicAckAsync(delivery.DeliveryTag, multiple: false, cancellationToken: ct);
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) when (ct.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        // Shutting down: leave the delivery unacked and the broker redelivers it.
    }
    catch
    {
        // A handler or interceptor threw. First failure gets one retry; a redelivered
        // failure goes to the dead-letter exchange instead of looping forever.
        await channel.BasicNackAsync(delivery.DeliveryTag, multiple: false,
            requeue: !delivery.Redelivered, cancellationToken: CancellationToken.None);
    }
}
```

**A scope per delivery.** Handlers resolve from the provider of the scope that dispatched
them, so a scope per delivery gives every message the same isolation an ASP.NET request
gets — scoped `DbContext`s and unit-of-work services just work. This is the shape the
dispatch path is optimized for: resolving `IEventMediator` builds a single facade object
over a shared dispatch engine, and the scoped-dispatch numbers on the
[performance page](/ergosfare.docs/preview/advanced/performance/) include exactly this cost.

**The runtime type decides the pipeline.** The variable is typed `IEvent`, so this takes
the interface-erased publish path: the pipeline is resolved from `@event.GetType()`, and
covariant handlers match as usual. Polymorphic dispatch at the boundary is first-class,
not a degraded mode.

**Transport metadata rides the context.** Anything put into `Settings.Items` is visible to
every handler and interceptor in the dispatch through the execution context:

```csharp
public sealed class OrderPlacedHandler : IEventHandler<OrderPlaced>
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(OrderPlaced @event, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        if (context.TryGet<bool>("rabbitmq:redelivered", out var redelivered) && redelivered)
        {
            // second attempt — be idempotent about side effects
        }

        return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

**Ack only after the pipeline.** `PublishAsync` completes when every matched handler and
interceptor has run, so the ack really means "processed". The nack policy is deliberately
boring: malformed and unknown messages never requeue, handler failures requeue exactly
once. Declare the queue with a dead-letter exchange so nacked deliveries land somewhere
inspectable instead of vanishing.

## Wiring

```csharp
builder.Services.AddErgosfare(o => o.AddEventModule(e => e.RegisterGenerated()));

builder.Services.AddSingleton<IConnectionFactory>(new ConnectionFactory { HostName = "localhost" });
builder.Services.AddSingleton(new WireEventRegistry(typeof(OrderPlaced), typeof(OrderCancelled)));

builder.Services.AddHostedService(sp => new RabbitMqEventConsumer(
    sp.GetRequiredService<IServiceScopeFactory>(),
    sp.GetRequiredService<WireEventRegistry>(),
    sp.GetRequiredService<IConnectionFactory>(),
    queueName: "orders"));
```

The events themselves are ordinary Ergosfare events — `RegisterGenerated()` discovers their
handlers at compile time like any other; nothing about arriving over a queue changes their
registration.

## Notes

* **No handlers is a silent success by default.** An event nobody handles completes
  normally and gets acked. If an unhandled wire event should be an error in your system,
  publish with `new EventMediationSettings { ThrowIfNoHandlerFound = true }` and the nack
  path takes over.
* **Ordering and concurrency.** One consumer processes deliveries sequentially. For
  parallelism, raise the channel's prefetch and run multiple consumers — per-delivery
  scopes already isolate them from each other.
* **Native AOT.** The dispatch side is AOT-clean (see
  [Native AOT and trimming](/ergosfare.docs/preview/advanced/aot-and-trimming/)), but
  `JsonSerializer.Deserialize(Type)` here is reflection-based serialization — on AOT,
  switch the envelope handling to a source-generated `JsonSerializerContext`.
